  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Severe weather and flash flooding are possible \nthrough tonight.\n\nVery active weather is expected over the next 12 to 24 hours, \nwith the threat for flash flooding, damaging wind gusts, and a \nfew tornadoes.\n\nAs of 10 AM, regional radar shows two well-defined MCVs. The\nfirst is centered over southwestern PA, with ongoing showers to\nthe south and east across much of our forecast area. This \nactivity will continue to spread eastward over the next few\nhours. A second MCV is located across northeastern Indiana and\nnorthwestern Ohio. A few thunderstorms are already ongoing ahead\nof this disturbance across Indiana and Ohio, and additional\nstorms are expected to pop up ahead of it this afternoon, and\nthen move in from the west this evening into the early overnight\nhours. Questions still remain regarding how much development of\nstorms will occur in-between these two disturbances. As of now,\nthere is isolated activity developing across central Ohio in \nthe clearing between the two systems. Recent HRRR runs try to \nhave this activity dissipate before making it into our forecast \narea, but that seems questionable given that there's full sun in\nplace ahead of it, which should allow it to move into an \nincreasingly unstable environment. If this activity were to hold\ntogether, it would likely make it to the Allegheny Front around\n4 PM and then track eastward across the area through the \nevening. The storms associated with the MCV in IN/OH currently \nshould reach the Alleghenies by around 8 PM, and then eventually\nclear the bay by around 2 or 3 AM.\n\nTrends will need to be very closely monitored given the very\nhigh PW airmass and potential for repeat/training thunderstorms.\nShould heavier thunderstorms train over any one area, 3 to 6 \ninches of rain would be possible on a localized basis. This \ncould cause scattered to numerous instances of flash flooding, \nwith considerable flash flooding possible in urban/poor drainage\nareas or areas that have seen heavy rain recently. If and when \nit becomes clearer where the greatest coverage of heavy rain \nwill set up, additional Flood Watches may be issued.\n\nRegarding the severe weather threat, strong low-level winds\nwill boost SRH in a high moisture content environment. Wet\ndownbursts and brief spin ups would be possible in this\nenvironment, with the tornado threat maximized along the slowly\nlifting warm frontal zone - especially if any cell or cluster\ninteracts with/tracks along it. Shear is favorable for right-\nmoving (i.e. NW to SE) supercells if enough instability is\nrealized, and in those cases storms would be more apt to ingest\nstreamwise vorticity along the warm front (or residual outflow).\n\nThe wave this evening, though timed a bit after peak heating,\nmay pose the relative highest risk of damaging wind gusts due \nto increased low-level flow orthogonal to what will probably a \nlinear type feature.\n\nConvection (and corresponding severe/flood threats) may wane\nonly gradually tonight with continued low-level moist advection\nbeneath height falls aloft. This causes instability to remain \nor even increase through the night.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Thunderstorm chances return Wednesday night into\nThursday.\n\nAs a cold front slowly moves across our area late Wednesday and \nThursday, shower and thunderstorm chances return across most of\nour forecast region. Friday and Saturday, models continue to \ndepict a stalled front lying in our south, bringing sustained \nchances for summer-time showers and thunderstorms. Currently, \nit's unclear if the position of synoptic features and \ninstability will be sufficient for hazardous weather. For now, \nthis will be a feature to monitor as the synoptic setup becomes \nmore defined. Temperatures may fluctuate a little depending on \nwhat side of the front we're on, but likely fall some by next \nweekend, assuming the front pushes to the southeast.",
